ProsperoSunshine: stop() and accept() take turns on the acceptor

The host stops its servers from another thread than the one that runs them: nvhttp's start() calls stop() on both
GameStream servers after the shutdown event, the web page's watcher thread while start() runs. stop() closed the
acceptor there while the server's thread, done with a connection, called async_accept() on the same acceptor from the
accept handler; asio allows neither on one object from two threads at once. The close freed the reactor's descriptor
state and cleared the acceptor's pointer to it between async_accept's check and its use, and the host crashed with a
null pointer in start_accept_op while it stopped (1 stop in 12 in a console round; host_restart connects to 47989 every
100 ms until it is closed). A -fsanitize=thread build of tests/deps/sws_server_test.cpp, case stop_while_accepting,
reports the race and hits the same SEGV in epoll_reactor::start_op without this patch, in 3 runs of 3, and neither with
it.

A mutex of its own (start() holds start_stop_mutex when it calls accept()) is held by stop() around close() and by both
accept()s around async_accept(), and accept() leaves a closed acceptor alone. The host's own accept() for the web page
(src/web/web_server.cpp) and Sunshine's SunshineHTTPSServer::accept (its patch 0044) take it too. stop() also shuts down
and cancels the open connections from its thread; the sanitizer names that as well, but it can't free anything: a
connection is only deleted after it has left the set that stop() holds locked, and nothing closes a connection's socket
before that.

